//! lotus/stereo_tremolo.rye — ALES176: the TREMOLO carried into stereo — ALES74's triangle-LFO-on-amplitude run on BOTH channels with the SAME swing (centre, depth, period), the rung that OPENS the stereo MODULATION class the way
//! ALES170's tone opened the stereo EQ class. The EQ / filter class (ALES170–175) shapes a signal's SPECTRUM by a fixed or swept coefficient; the modulation family points a moving LFO at a target and lets it swing over time. ALES74
//! built the thinnest such rung for a mono Clip — the tremolo, the oldest amplitude effect there is: a triangle LFO drives the GAIN, so a steady note gently rises and falls in loudness. It is the thinnest because every output sample
//! depends only on ITS OWN input sample (never a neighbour), so it writes in place with no snapshot, no delay line, no lookahead. That thinness is exactly why it opens the stereo modulation class: with no carried state and no
//! backward read, a stereo tremolo is the cleanest possible per-channel pass.
//!
//! The crux — the SAME LFO on BOTH channels is the classic LINKED (auto-)tremolo: both speakers pulse together, the image held. A tremolo's swing (centre, depth, period) is scaled units the caller NAMES, not scalars measured across
//! the field, so the same swing on each channel preserves the stereo image for free — no linking machinery, no measurement. Because each output sample is its own input times the SHARED gain g(k) at that index, an identical-channel
//! (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical (the same input through the same LFO is the same output), and a PANNED master keeps its pan exactly: both sides are scaled by the same g(k), so the left:right ratio at every sample is
//! untouched — the tremolo pulses the whole field without wandering the image (an offset-phase stereo tremolo, which DOES wander the image, is a later rung; this opener is the linked one). Like the stateless EQ shelf/notch/bell and
//! unlike the band-pass, there is no carried state to thread across a seam.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadPeriod (a period not a positive multiple of four, or wider than max_clip), BadDepth (a negative depth or a floor below silence), BadGain (a top above unity — the tremolo attenuates, never boosts),
//! and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE either channel is touched — the swing and span validated once against the shared length up front (ALES74's own `precheck`, factored out for this), so a refusal never tremolos
//! one channel and leaves the other torn; then run ALES74's `tremolo` on each channel with the SAME swing. Unity gain (centre = glide_scale, depth = 0) passes both byte for byte; the gain is held to an attenuation, so the per-sample
//! saturate is a proven no-op and no tremolo voice is ever louder than the dry it scales, per channel.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO TREMOLO LAW: each channel equals ALES74's mono `tremolo` with the SAME swing over the same span byte for byte, proven side by side with genuinely different per-channel content — the left tremoloed
//! by the left's samples, the right by the right's, never crossed. THE IMAGE LAW: an identical-channel master stays identical through the tremolo (a mono-in-stereo signal never splits), AND a panned pair keeps its left:right ratio at
//! every sample (both sides scaled by the same shared gain — the linked tremolo pulses without wandering the image). THE ATTENUATION LAW: unity gain passes both channels byte for byte even at the rail, and no tremolo voice exceeds
//! the dry it scales on either channel (the gain an attenuation, the saturate a no-op). THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: an all-silent master tremolos to silence on both; left.len == right.len after; a bad period,
//! a bad depth, a bad gain, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES176.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-082400_fill-ales176-lotus-stereo-tremolo.md
//! Stands on: lotus/tremolo.rye (ALES74) — the mono tremolo run once per channel, its TremoloError reused whole and its `precheck` reused for the up-front atomic validation; lotus/glide.rye (ALES70) — glide_scale, the fixed-point
//! unit (256 = unity gain); lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip and the one true saturate.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each tremoloed through ALES74's own attenuating triangle gain (the triangle and multiply in i64, the write saturating once as a proven
//! no-op), siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only, never a length; the two channels leave balanced. The gain is counted in scaled units (256 = unity) and the period in samples, not hertz (a real time base is a later rung).
//! No delay line, no snapshot, no lookahead, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_tremolo.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const glide = @import("glide.rye");
const tremolo = @import("tremolo.rye");

pub const stereo_tremolo_version = "20260815.082400";

/// The stereo tremolo's faults are exactly ALES74's own `TremoloError` — `BadPeriod`, `BadDepth`, `BadGain`, and `BadRange` — reused by name, since carrying the tremolo into stereo adds no new fault: it validates one shared swing
/// and span against the shared length, then runs the same mono tremolo on each channel.
pub const StereoTremoloError = tremolo.TremoloError;

/// Apply a tremolo over `count` samples starting at `start` in BOTH channels of `sc` with the SAME swing — a triangle LFO of `depth_scaled` round `centre_scaled` over `period` samples driving the scaled GAIN — by running ALES74's
/// proven mono `tremolo` on each channel. The shared swing preserves the stereo image: an identical-channel master stays identical, and a panned master keeps its pan (both sides scaled by the same gain). Validates the swing and span
/// ONCE against the shared length before either channel is mutated (ALES74's `precheck`): refuses BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadGain, and BadRange, both channels untouched. Values only — never the length; the two channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_tremolo(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    centre_scaled: i64,
    depth_scaled: i64,
    period: u32,
) StereoTremoloError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared swing preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: the swing and span are legal against the SHARED length — checked before any write, so a refusal never tremolos one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    try tremolo.precheck(len, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period);

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels (same swing, same span, shared length), so each mono tremolo is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME LFO on each
    // channel, each scaling its own samples by the shared gain.
    try tremolo.tremolo(&sc.left, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period);
    try tremolo.tremolo(&sc.right, start, count, centre_scaled, depth_scaled, period);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — a tremolo writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO TREMOLO LAW — each channel equals ALES74's mono tremolo with the SAME swing byte for byte, different per-channel content; THE IMAGE LAW — an identical-channel master staying identical, a panned pair
//     keeping its ratio; THE ATTENUATION LAW — unity passthrough exact on both, no voice louder than the dry; THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — silence to silence, balanced, each refusal untouched, count 0 identity

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn abs_i16(x: i16) i64 {
    const v: i64 = @as(i64, x);
    return if (v < 0) -v else v;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const scale = glide.glide_scale;
    const centre: i64 = @divTrunc(scale, 2);
    const depth: i64 = @divTrunc(scale, 2);

    // 1. THE STEREO TREMOLO LAW — each channel equals ALES74's mono tremolo with the SAME swing, proven side by side over one span with genuinely different per-channel content: the left tremoloed by the left's samples, the right by
    //    the right's, never crossed.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400, 6000, -6000 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_tremolo(&sc, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &ll);
        try tremolo.tremolo(&ml, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &rr);
        try tremolo.tremolo(&mr, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftTremoloNotMono;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightTremoloNotMono;
        // The two channels genuinely differ — the left is not the right tremoloed, proving no crossing.
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.ChannelsDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 2. THE IMAGE LAW — an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical through the tremolo: the same input through the same LFO is the same deterministic output, so the two channels never split.
    {
        const same = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000, 300, -300 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &same, &same);
        try stereo_tremolo(&sc, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 2b. THE IMAGE LAW, panned — a panned pair (the right a fixed fraction of the left) keeps its left:right ratio at every sample, because both sides are scaled by the SAME gain g(k). The linked tremolo pulses the whole field
    //     without wandering the image: wherever the pre-tremolo pair held 2·right == left, the post-tremolo pair holds it too (within one unit of integer rounding, checked as an exact match on the doubled right).
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 12000, -4000, 16000, -16000, 2000, -2000 };
        var rr: [8]i16 = undefined;
        var j: u32 = 0;
        while (j < 8) : (j += 1) rr[j] = @intCast(@divTrunc(@as(i64, ll[j]), 2)); // right is exactly half the left — a pan
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_tremolo(&sc, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8);
        // Each side scaled by the same g(k): left'' = left·g/scale, right'' = (left/2)·g/scale. Since left is even and g·left is divisible cleanly here, 2·right'' == left'' holds sample for sample — the pan is preserved.
        const lo = sc.left.samples();
        const ro = sc.right.samples();
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < 8) : (k += 1) if (2 * @as(i64, ro[k]) != @as(i64, lo[k])) return error.PanRatioWandered;
    }

    // 3. THE ATTENUATION LAW — unity gain (centre = scale, depth = 0) passes both channels byte for byte even at the rail (the saturate a no-op), and no tremolo voice exceeds the dry it scales on either channel.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 32767, -32768, 1000, -1000, 20000, -20000, 5, -5 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -32768, 32767, -1000, 1000, -20000, 20000, -5, 5 };
        var pass = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&pass, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_tremolo(&pass, 0, 8, scale, 0, 8); // unity: centre = scale, depth = 0
        if (!eq(pass.left.samples(), &ll) or !eq(pass.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.UnityNotPassthrough;

        var att = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&att, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_tremolo(&att, 0, 8, centre, depth, 8);
        var k: u32 = 0;
        while (k < 8) : (k += 1) {
            if (abs_i16(att.left.samples()[k]) > abs_i16(ll[k])) return error.LeftVoiceLouderThanDry;
            if (abs_i16(att.right.samples()[k]) > abs_i16(rr[k])) return error.RightVoiceLouderThanDry;
        }
    }

    // 4a. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — an all-silent master tremolos to silence on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_tremolo(&sc, 0, 4, centre, depth, 4);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 200, -9000 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000 });
        try stereo_tremolo(&bc, 0, 4, centre, depth, 4);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 4) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 4b. THE ATOMICITY LAW — a bad period (not a multiple of four), a bad depth (floor below silence), a bad gain (top above unity), and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still
    //     balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7, -8 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5, 4 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_tremolo(&a, 0, 4, centre, depth, 6)) |_| return error.BadPeriodSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadPeriod) return error.WrongBadPeriod;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.PeriodRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_tremolo(&b, 0, 4, centre, scale, 4)) |_| return error.BadDepthSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadDepth) return error.WrongBadDepth; // floor = centre − scale < 0
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.DepthRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_tremolo(&c, 0, 4, scale, depth, 4)) |_| return error.BadGainSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadGain) return error.WrongBadGain; // top = scale + depth > scale
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.GainRefusalMutated;

        var d = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&d, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_tremolo(&d, 1, 4, centre, depth, 4)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(d.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(d.right.samples(), &tr) or d.left.len != d.right.len) return error.RangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 4c. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no sample to tremolo.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_tremolo(&noop, 1, 0, centre, depth, 4); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-tremolo: the TREMOLO CARRIED INTO STEREO — ALES74's triangle-LFO-on-amplitude run on BOTH channels with the SAME swing (centre, depth, period), the rung that OPENS the stereo MODULATION class the way " ++
        "ALES170's tone opened the stereo EQ class. The EQ / filter class shapes a spectrum by a coefficient; the modulation family points a moving LFO at a target and lets it swing over time. The tremolo is the thinnest such rung — " ++
        "a triangle LFO drives the GAIN, so a steady note gently rises and falls in loudness — thinnest because every output sample depends only on its own input sample, so it writes in place with no snapshot, no delay line, no " ++
        "lookahead. The SAME LFO on both channels is the classic LINKED (auto-)tremolo: both speakers pulse together, the image held. A tremolo's swing is scaled units the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the " ++
        "same swing on each channel preserves the stereo image for free; because each output is its own input times the shared gain g(k), an identical-channel master stays identical and a panned master keeps its pan exactly (the " ++
        "left:right ratio untouched — the tremolo pulses without wandering the image; an offset-phase stereo tremolo is a later rung). It validates the swing and span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated " ++
        "(BadPeriod, BadDepth, BadGain, BadRange; ALES74's precheck reused), so a refusal never tremolos one channel and leaves the other torn; TremoloError reused whole. THE STEREO TREMOLO LAW: each channel equals ALES74's mono " ++
        "tremolo with the same swing byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing. THE IMAGE LAW: an identical-channel master staying identical, a panned pair keeping its left:right ratio at every sample. THE ATTENUATION LAW: " ++
        "unity gain passing both byte for byte even at the rail, no voice louder than the dry on either channel. THE BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: silence staying silence on both, left.len == right.len after, a bad " ++
        "period / a bad depth / a bad gain / an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips tremoloed through the proven " ++
        "mono tremolo's attenuating triangle gain, the triangle and multiply in i64 and the write saturating once as a no-op, the gain in scaled units not hertz; no delay line, no snapshot, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, " ++
        "no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
